      <MissionDesc>Groundwork San Diego-Chollas Creek works at the intersection of social, environmental, and climate justice, grounded in strong university and community partnerships, to deliver education, green infrastructure, and climate safe neighborhoods. Groundworks mission is to bring about the regeneration, improvement and management of the environment through community-based partnerships which empower people, businesses and organizations to promote economic and social well-being. Groundwork is committed to revitalizing the Chollas Creek Watershed and its communities. Groundwork San Diego has spent 17 years building trusted relationships in the Chollas Creek Watershed through environmental and climate justice initiatives led by and for the community. Our flagship EarthLab Climate Action Center, developed on previously vacant land at Millennial Tech Middle School, has educated thousands of students and families about environmental science and climate resilience. We maintain active partnerships wit</MissionDesc>
      <SignificantNewProgramSrvcInd>false</SignificantNewProgramSrvcInd>
      <SignificantChangeInd>false</SignificantChangeInd>
      <ExpenseAmt>1313036</ExpenseAmt>
      <Desc>Watershed InfrastructurePlanning, design, and construction of community-driven climate resilience projects are a central component of Groundworks work in the Chollas Creek Watershed. One of only a select group of NGOs approved to deliver capital projects on and improve City assets, Groundwork has generated over $10M in funding to restore and transform Chollas Creek and support its neighborhoods with climate-resilient parks; permeable -surface active transportation bikeways; expanded tree canopies. These projects aim to reconnect communities through climate-resilient infrastructure and honor the Kumeyaay history in the watershed through cultural signage. EducationThe Groundwork EarthLab Educators deliver place-based learning experiences and projects during the school day to over 2000 K-12 youth of the San Diego Unified School Districts Lincoln Cluster of Schools. EarthLab Educators connect real-world challenges that Groundwork tackles within the Chollas Creek Watershed with learning standards to empower students to focus on their studies and leverage their knowledge to affect change in their communities. Climate-safe NeighborhoodsThe Chollas Creek Watershed exemplifies compounded impacts of historic environmental injustice. 1930s creek channelization began a pattern of infrastructure decisions that overlooked community wellbeing. 1950s freeway construction further divided neighborhoods, displacing hundreds of families and businesses, particularly BIPOC residents who had rare opportunities for property ownership in Southeastern San Diego. Today, 80+% of residents are BIPOC, and CalEnviroScreen places census tracts in the 90th-98th percentiles for environmental burdens. Unlike more affluent neighborhoods, this area lacks adequate green infrastructure to absorb stormwater and provide shade. January 2024 floods displaced hundreds of residents, dramatically illustrating how historical disinvestment magnifies climate impacts.</Desc>
